  "ajanta-ellora-caves-tour-from-aurangabad": {
    "overview": "Want to see India\u2019s glorious past? Then head straight to Ajanta & Ellora Caves, which will give you a taste of history and art in one bite! See More\n\nThese UNESCO World Heritage sites located in Maharashtra are famous for their ancient paintings and rock-cut caves that date back to the second century BC. While Ajanta Caves give you glimpses into the life of Buddha, Ellora Caves, on the other hand, show you the yesteryear\u2019s Hindu, Buddhist, and Jain architecture!Book our 3 Days/2 Nights Ajanta Ellora tour package to see the remarkable beauty of these historical attractions. With our tour plan, you get to enjoy premium accommodation, delicious meals, and dedicated transportation with a professional driver. In addition, this trip package includes sightseeing at Bibi Ka Maqbara, Panchakki, and Daulatabad Fort as well. Contact us now to go on a memorable tour that will stay etched in your memory for a long time!See Less\n\nThese UNESCO World Heritage sites located in Maharashtra are famous for their ancient paintings and rock-cut caves that date back to the second century BC. While Ajanta Caves give you glimpses into the life of Buddha, Ellora Caves, on the other hand, show you the yesteryear\u2019s Hindu, Buddhist, and Jain architecture!\n\nBook our 3 Days/2 Nights Ajanta Ellora tour package to see the remarkable beauty of these historical attractions. With our tour plan, you get to enjoy premium accommodation, delicious meals, and dedicated transportation with a professional driver. In addition, this trip package includes sightseeing at Bibi Ka Maqbara, Panchakki, and Daulatabad Fort as well. Contact us now to go on a memorable tour that will stay etched in your memory for a long time!",
    "highlights": [
      "Visit to Ajanta Caves, which consists of 30 rock-hewn Buddhist caves that feature chaityas (chapels) and Viharas (monasteries)",
      "Visit to Ellora Caves that have 34 cave temples with the grand Kailasa Temple as the highlight",
      "Explore Bibi ka Maqbara, which is often called the \u201cTaj Mahal of the Deccan\u201d",
      "Visit to Panchakki \u2013 an ancient water mill",
      "Discover the Aurangabad Caves that reflect Tantric influences",
      "Trip to Daulatabad Fort, which is a historic hill fortress located near Aurangabad",
      "See More"
    ],
    "itinerary": [
      {
        "title": "Day 1: Arrival at Aurangabad",
        "description": "Upon your arrival in Aurangabad, you\u2019ll be greeted by our team representative at Airport/Railway station.\nYou\u2019ll be taken to your pre-booked hotel.\nRelax for a while, then enjoy a sightseeing tour in Aurangabad, consisting of Bibi Ka Maqbara, Daulatabad Fort, and Aurangabad Caves.\nEnjoy the overnight stay at the hotel.\nAurangabad:\nThe city of Aurangabad was founded in 1610, on the site of a village, Khirki by Malik Ambar - the Prime Minister of Murtaza Nizam Shah II. When Fateh Khan, Malik Ambar's son turned successor in 1626, he gave the city the name 'Fatehpur'.\nAurangabad today is a bustling city of Maharashtra with diverse big and small industries, fine silken textiles, and exquisite hand woven brocades of silver and gold fabrics, Himroo of the world fame.\nTo scholars and lovers of art and culture the city is more familiar as the gateway to the ancient caves of Ajanta and Ellora, both famous as treasure houses of Indian Art and Sculpture.\nAurangabad Caves:\nThe almost forgotten caves of Aurangabad lie just outside the city. Excavated between the 2nd and 6th century AD, they reflect TANTRIC influences in their iconography and architectural designs. In all there are nine caves which are mainly Viharas (monasteries).\nOvernight stay at the hotel.\nPlaces You\u2019ll See"
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 2: Aurangabad Sightseeing Tour",
        "description": "Have a hearty breakfast and then get ready for a sightseeing tour!\nVisit Ajanta Caves & Ellora Caves.\nReturn to the hotel for an overnight stay.\nAjanta caves:\nNestling in an inner fold of the Sahyadri hills, 100 km from Aurangabad in the shape of a mammoth horse- shoe, are the 30 rock-hewn caves of Ajanta. The Caves date from the 2nd century BC. Discovered in 1819 by a group of British army officers, these startling achievements took around 600 years to create.\nCarved with little more than a hammer and chisel, Ajanta, once the retreat of Buddhist monastic orders features several 'Chaityas' (chapels) and 'Viharas' (monasteries). Return to Aurangabad and overnight will be at Aurangabad.\nEllora Caves:\nImpressive in their own right are the rock-hewn temples and monasteries of Ellora that lie just 30 km away from Aurangabad city. In all, there are 34 cave temples, 12 Mahayana Buddhist caves (550-750 AD), 17 Hindu caves (600-875 AD), and 5 caves of the Jain faith (800-1000 AD). Kailas Temple (cave16), the central attraction at Ellora, is the most remarkable.\nIf time permits - then visit the Bhadra Maruti Temple (only temple where Lord Hanuman is seen in a sleeping posture).\nPlaces You\u2019ll See"
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 3: Aurangabad Departure",
        "description": "Have breakfast in the morning, then check out of your hotel.\nNow, you\u2019ll be dropped off at Aurangabad airport/ Railway Station by our team member.\nThis concludes your trip with us. Hope you had a nice tour with us!"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": []
  },
  "ajanta-ellora-caves-tour": {
    "overview": "This tour essentially packs the unique flavor of culture, taste, history and art with a refreshing doze of sights and serenity. If you are looking for a week out and nurture a love for history then, refresh your mind and body with this tour.\n\nThis tour touches two interesting cities Mumbai and Aurangabad and an archeological place of international importance, the caves of Ajanta and Ellora. While Mumbai has a cosmopolitan culture Aurangabad still retains its Muslim heritage in architecture and taste. But as you step into the caves of Ajanta and Ellora, there is a feeling that you have arrived into an altogether different era.\n\nThis tour touches two interesting cities Mumbai and Aurangabad and an archeological place of international importance, the caves of Ajanta and Ellora. While Mumbai has a cosmopolitan culture Aurangabad still retains its Muslim heritage in architecture and taste. But as you step into the caves of Ajanta and Ellora, there is a feeling that you have arrived into an altogether different era.\n\nAjanta Ellora Caves Tour Itinerary Package\n\n\nDay 1 :Arrival at Mumbai\n\n\nThe tour begins with your arrival in Mumbai. Our My Quick Trippers representative will meet you at the airport and take you to your hotel. You can spend the day as you wish to soak in the flavors of Mumbai. You can grab a bite at any Parsee eatery, take a stroll down the Marine Drive, watch a Hindi movie or see the famous film studios and houses of your favorite film stars be it Amitabh Bachchan, Shah Rukh Khan or cricketing God Sachin Tendulkar. While it may not be possible to know Mumbai in a day, do save some energy to see the city transform itself into a beauty at night. Take rest before stepping out on a guided tour the next morning.\n\n\n\n\nDay 2 :Guided Tour of Mumbai\n\n\nAfter breakfast proceed for a guided tour of the major attractions in Mumbai. Kamla Nehru Park and Hanging Gardens situated on the slopes of Malabar Hill, offering a panoramic view of Marine drive & Chowpatty Beach, Prince of Wales Museum, Mani Bhavan, Dhobi Ghat, Gateway of India and drive through the Crawford market, Marine Drive & Flora Fountain. We suggest you have the famous snacks like Vada Pav, bhel puri, gol gappa and Nariyal Pani before you end the city tour of Mumbai. Retire for the night in the luxury of your hotel in Mumbai.\n\n\n\n\nDay 3 :Mumbai \u2013 Aurangabad\n\n\nA flight from Mumbai to Aurangabad takes around 1 hr. We will transfer you to the airport at Mumbai and again from airport to your hotel in Aurangabad. You can explore this historical city on your own for the rest of the day visiting beautiful places like Bibi ka Maqbara which is an imitation of the Taj Mahal in Agra, the Panchakki \u2013 a 17th-century water mill, Salim Ali Lake and bird sanctuary while savouring its famous Naan Qalia.\n\n\n\n\nDay 4 :Aurangabad \u2013 Ajanta \u2013 Ellora\n\n\nAfter breakfast we take you for a full day tour of Ajanta and Ellora Caves which have been declared as the World Heritage Sites by UNESCO. Ajanta Caves are 106 kms from Aurangabad and date back to the 2nd century BC. The 30 rock cut caves are adorned with Buddhist sculpture and the frescoes portray in epic form the religious and secular life through eight centuries. Ellora Caves which are at a distance of around 29 kms encompass 34 rock cut shrines representing Buddhist, Hindu & Jain art dating from the 4th to 9th century A.D. The most remarkable among them is the Kailash Temple, meant to be a replica of Lord Shiva\u2019s celestial abode \u2013 Kailash is the best example of Rock cut architecture and an engineering marvel. Return to your hotel and rest for the night.\n\n\n\n\nDay 5 :Aurangabad \u2013 Mumbai \u2013 Departure\n\n\nWe will transfer you to airport for flight to Mumbai and again take you to the international airport for an onward flight. But if you have a mind and some time, do bargain and shop some collectibles from Mumbai Chor bazaar \u2013 the thief\u2019s market.",
    "highlights": [
      "City tour of Mumbai",
      "The historical city of Aurangabad, visit to Bibi ka Maqbara and the Panchakki - a 17th-century water mill, Salim Ali Lake and bird sanctuary while savouring its famous Naan Qalia.",
      "Full day tour of Ajanta and Ellora caves"
    ],
    "itinerary": [
      {
        "title": "Day 1 :Arrival at Mumbai",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 2 :Guided Tour of Mumbai",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 3 :Mumbai \u2013 Aurangabad",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 4 :Aurangabad \u2013 Ajanta \u2013 Ellora",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 5 :Aurangabad \u2013 Mumbai \u2013 Departure",
        "description": ""
      }
    ],
    "faqs": []
  },
