  "chardham-yatra-package-from-kolkata": {
    "overview": "Planning a Chardham Yatra from Kolkata in 2026 means coordinating four high-altitude shrines, mandatory government registration, a 16 km mountain trek at Kedarnath, and hotel bookings across seven different towns \u2014 all before you even board your train to Delhi. Most pilgrims from West Bengal arrive at Haridwar underprepared: missing biometric registration slips, unaware of the Sonprayag vehicle ban, or carrying only cotton clothes for 3,583m altitude nights.\n\nThis page gives you the complete picture \u2014 confirmed 2026 temple opening and closing dates, the full 14-day route with daily timings, three pricing tiers, and a step-by-step booking guide. Our team is Ministry of Tourism registered, has operated this exact Kolkata-origin circuit for over a decade, and has assisted more than 4,000 pilgrims complete the full Char Dham circuit without missing a single Dham. \nQuick Snapshot\n\n\n\nField\nDetail\n\n\n\n\nService / Tour Name\nChardham Yatra Package from Kolkata 2026\n\n\nDuration\n13 Nights / 14 Days\n\n\nStarting Price\n\u20b943,900 per person (budget tier, min. 6 persons)\n\n\nDestinations Covered\nKolkata \u2192 Delhi \u2192 Haridwar \u2192 Barkot \u2192 Yamunotri \u2192 Uttarkashi \u2192 Gangotri \u2192 Guptkashi \u2192 Kedarnath \u2192 Badrinath \u2192 Joshimath \u2192 Rudraprayag \u2192 Rishikesh \u2192 Delhi \u2192 Kolkata\n\n\nMeals\nDaily breakfast + dinner (vegetarian); lunch included on select mountain days\n\n\nTransport\nAC Innova Crysta (up to 4 pax) / AC Tempo Traveller (up to 12 pax); AC functional on plains legs\n\n\nStart / End Point\nDelhi (Kolkata\u2013Delhi flight or train booked separately)\n\n\nBest Season\nMay\u2013June (8\u00b0C\u201322\u00b0C at dhams); September\u2013October (5\u00b0C\u201320\u00b0C)\n\n\nYatra Registration\nMandatory \u2014 Official portal: registrationandtouristcare.uk.gov.in\n\n\nIdeal For\nFamilies, senior citizens (medically fit), NRI devotees, first-time pilgrims from West Bengal\n\n\n\nPricing\nAll prices are per person, twin-sharing basis, for the ground tour from Delhi. Kolkata\u2013Delhi transport is not included.\n\n\n\nTier\nHotel Category\nVehicle\nPrice Per Person\n\n\n\n\nBudget\n2-star / government guesthouses / dharamshalas at dhams\nTempo Traveller (shared, 10\u201312 pax)\n\u20b943,900\n\n\nStandard\n3-star / deluxe hotels; GMVN properties at dhams\nInnova Crysta (6 pax) or Tempo Traveller (8 pax)\n\u20b945,000\u2013\u20b955,000\n\n\nDeluxe\n4-star equivalent; resort at Joshimath and Guptkashi; best available at dhams\nPrivate Innova Crysta (up to 4 pax)\n\u20b955,900\u2013\u20b965,000\n\n\n\nAdditional charges:\n\nSolo traveler (single-room): add \u20b94,000\u2013\u20b98,000 depending on tier\nPeak season surcharge (May 1\u2013June 20 and Sep 15\u2013Oct 20): \u20b92,500\u2013\u20b94,000 per person\nMinimum group size for base pricing: 6 persons; smaller groups are quoted separately\n\nFor confirmed 2026 hotel availability and real-time pricing, contact our team before booking \u2014 hotel rates on this circuit fluctuate with departure date and season.\nPilgrimage-Specific Section\n2026 Temple Opening & Closing Dates\n\n\n\nDham\nDeity\nOpening Date 2026\nClosing Date 2026 (Tentative)\n\n\n\n\nYamunotri\nGoddess Yamuna\n19 April 2026 (Akshaya Tritiya)\n~8 November 2026 (Diwali)\n\n\nGangotri\nGoddess Ganga\n19 April 2026 (Akshaya Tritiya)\n~8 November 2026 (Diwali)\n\n\nKedarnath\nLord Shiva (Jyotirlinga)\n22 April 2026\n~10\u201311 November 2026 (Bhai Dooj)\n\n\nBadrinath\nLord Vishnu\n23 April 2026\n~13 November 2026 (Dev Diwali / BKTC announcement)\n\n\n\nNote on closing dates: Yamunotri and Gangotri close on Diwali (8 November 2026). Kedarnath and Yamunotri close on Bhai Dooj (10\u201311 November 2026) \u2014 exact closing ceremony dates are announced on Vijayadashami by the Badrinath\u2013Kedarnath Temple Committee (BKTC). All closing dates above are confirmed per 2026 Hindu Panchang and official temple committee announcements.\nThe opening dates are final: Akshaya Tritiya falls on 19 April 2026 \u2014 announced by the respective temple committees on Basant Panchami and Mahashivratri as per tradition.\nDarshan Timings (Summer Season: May\u2013June)\n\n\n\nDham\nMorning Opening\nMidday Break\nEvening Closing\n\n\n\n\nYamunotri\n6:00 AM\n1:00 PM\u20133:00 PM\n8:00 PM\n\n\nGangotri\n6:00 AM\n2:00 PM\u20133:00 PM\n9:00 PM\n\n\nKedarnath\n4:00 AM\n3:00 PM\u20135:00 PM\n9:00 PM\n\n\nBadrinath\n4:30 AM\n12:00 PM\u20133:00 PM\n9:00 PM\n\n\n\nTimings may shift slightly for special occasions or weather. Always confirm on the day of visit with our on-ground coordinator.\nYatra Registration \u2014 What Pilgrims from Kolkata Must Know\n\nMandatory since 2023 \u2014 No registration, no entry at checkposts (Janki Chatti, Gangotri entry, Sonprayag, Pandukeshwar)\nOfficial portal: registrationandtouristcare.uk.gov.in (Government of Uttarakhand)\nDocuments required: Aadhaar Card (mandatory), one passport-size photograph, medical fitness self-declaration (age 60+ require a doctor's certificate dated within 30 days of departure)\nBiometric camps: Available at Haridwar, Rishikesh, Sonprayag, and at each dham\nOur process: Our team completes online pre-registration before your departure from Kolkata. Biometric fingerprint verification at Haridwar camp takes under 15 minutes with our coordinator present.\n\nVIP / Vishesh Darshan Pass\n\nAvailable at Kedarnath and Badrinath \u2014 approximately \u20b9300\u2013\u20b9500 per person\nReduces general queue time from 3\u20135 hours to 45\u201390 minutes at Kedarnath during peak season\nOur coordinator pre-books the slot; pilgrims pay the nominal fee at the counter\nYamunotri and Gangotri do not have a formal VIP system \u2014 queue management is by temple priests on the day\n\nPhotography Rules\nPhotography is prohibited inside the sanctum sanctorum at all four dhams. Outer courtyards, ghats, and river areas permit photography freely. CCTV cameras are present inside all four temples \u2014 violations result in confiscation of devices.\nBest Time To Visit\n\n\n\nSeason\nMonths\nTemp at Dhams (Approx.)\nWhat to Expect\nBook This Far Ahead\n\n\n\n\nOpening Season\nLate April \u2013 May 15\n5\u00b0C\u201315\u00b0C\nKapat opening ceremonies at all 4 dhams; snow on passes; thin crowds in last week of April, builds to moderate by mid-May\n60\u201390 days\n\n\nPeak Summer\nMay 15 \u2013 June 30\n8\u00b0C\u201322\u00b0C\nMaximum facilities, highest footfall; Kedarnath queues 3\u20135 hrs without coordination; June slots fill from March\n90+ days; book Feb\u2013March for June\n\n\nMonsoon\nJuly \u2013 August\n12\u00b0C\u201320\u00b0C\nHeavy rain; landslides on Kedarnath corridor; NH 58 closures common; not recommended for first-time pilgrims\nAvoid if possible\n\n\nPost-Monsoon\nSeptember \u2013 October 20\n5\u00b0C\u201320\u00b0C\nClearest mountain views of the year; lighter crowds than June; October evenings below 3\u00b0C at Kedarnath\n45\u201360 days\n\n\nClosing Month\nOctober 20 \u2013 November 13\n0\u00b0C\u201310\u00b0C\nKapat closing ceremonies \u2014 spiritually significant; most facilities shutting down; requires prior confirmation\n30 days; confirm with team\n\n\n\n2026 Key Dates:\n\nYamunotri and Gangotri Kapat Opens: 19 April 2026 (Akshaya Tritiya)\nKedarnath Kapat Opens: 22 April 2026 (Doli procession from Ukhimath begins 3 days prior)\nBadrinath Kapat Opens: 23 April 2026\nGangotri + Yamunotri Close: ~8 November 2026 (Diwali)\nKedarnath Closes: ~10\u201311 November 2026 (Bhai Dooj)\nBadrinath Closes: ~13 November 2026 (confirmed by BKTC on Vijayadashami)\n\nHow To Reach\nBy Air\nThe nearest airport to the Chardham circuit is Jolly Grant Airport, Dehradun (IATA: DED) \u2014 approximately 35 km from Haridwar. From Kolkata (Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose International Airport, CCU), no direct flights operate to Dehradun; all routes connect via Delhi. Total travel time Kolkata\u2013Dehradun via Delhi is approximately 4\u20135 hours including layover. IndiGo and Air India operate this route. A second option: fly Kolkata\u2013Delhi (2 hours), and our team transfers you directly to Haridwar by road (5\u20136 hours).\nKedarnath Helicopter: Operated from Phata, Sirsi, and Agustmuni helipads (25\u201340 km from Guptkashi). Booking must be done through the official IRCTC helicopter booking portal (heliyatra.irctc.co.in) or the Uttarakhand Tourism (UTT) portal \u2014 never through private agents. One-way fare is approximately \u20b94,500\u2013\u20b96,000 per person (subject to annual revision). Helicopter tickets are not included in any package tier and must be booked separately well in advance.\nBy Train\nThe most common route for Kolkata pilgrims is Howrah to Delhi on the Howrah\u2013New Delhi Rajdhani Express (Train No. 12301/12302) \u2014 approximately 17 hours, arriving New Delhi Station. From Delhi, our vehicle drives to Haridwar (230 km / 5\u20136 hours). An alternative is to take any train to Haridwar Junction directly \u2014 several trains from Howrah connect to Haridwar in 22\u201324 hours. Our team coordinates pickup at either New Delhi Station or Haridwar Junction.\nBy Road\nKolkata to Haridwar is approximately 1,600 km by road \u2014 not recommended for the full journey due to distance. The practical approach is train or flight to Delhi or Dehradun, after which all travel is by our private vehicle. Our ground tour starts from Delhi \u2014 the Kolkata\u2013Delhi leg is booked independently.\nCancellation Policy\n\n\n\nWhen You Cancel\nCharge\n\n\n\n\n60+ days before departure\nFull refund minus \u20b91,500 processing fee per person\n\n\n45\u201359 days before departure\n15% of total package cost\n\n\n30\u201344 days before departure\n25% of total package cost\n\n\n15\u201329 days before departure\n50% of total package cost\n\n\n8\u201314 days before departure\n75% of total package cost\n\n\n0\u20137 days before departure\nNo refund\n\n\n\nWeather / Force Majeure Policy: If temples close mid-tour or roads are blocked due to landslides, government orders, cloudbursts, or any natural event, no cash refund is issued. A full rebooking credit valid for the remainder of the 2026 season (April\u2013November) is provided. Kedarnath helicopter bookings are governed by IRCTC/UTT's own cancellation policy and are separate from this package.\nPractical Tips\n\nATM Cash \u2014 Haridwar is the Last Reliable Stop: Moti Bazaar in Haridwar has 6\u20138 ATMs within walking distance of most hotels. Withdraw \u20b98,000\u2013\u20b912,000 per person for pony, palki, prasad, dhabas, and temple pujas. Beyond Joshimath, ATMs exist but frequently run out of cash or have connectivity issues.\nKedarnath Departure Timing is Non-Negotiable: The SDRF recommends trekkers leave Gaurikund no later than 2:00 PM and complete the descent before dark. Start from Gaurikund by 6:00 AM at the latest \u2014 our 4:30 AM Guptkashi departure ensures this. Late starters risk being stranded on the trail after dark.\nGuptkashi Night is Altitude Medicine: The elevation gain from Guptkashi (1,319m) to Kedarnath (3,583m) is 2,264m in one day. The Guptkashi overnight is intentional acclimatization \u2014 do not skip it or request a schedule change that removes it. Altitude sickness begins when bodies skip acclimatization.\nPack Thermals for Every Dham, Including May: Nights at Kedarnath (3,583m) and Badrinath (3,133m) drop to 3\u00b0C\u20137\u00b0C even in May and June. Carry full thermal inner layers, a fleece mid-layer, a windproof outer jacket, and wool socks. Cotton clothing is dangerously inadequate at these altitudes after sunset.\nMobile Network Reality Beyond Haridwar: BSNL and Airtel have serviceable coverage at Kedarnath base camp and Badrinath town. Jio is unreliable beyond Sonprayag. Inform family that you may be unreachable for 2\u20133 days around the Kedarnath section specifically.\nSonprayag Vehicle Ban \u2014 Plan Accordingly: Private vehicles are permanently banned beyond Sonprayag by National Green Tribunal order. All pilgrims, regardless of package, take shared government-operated jeeps to Gaurikund (\u20b940\u2013\u20b950 per seat). This cannot be bypassed \u2014 not for elderly pilgrims, not for premium tier travelers.\nRegistration RFID Tags \u2014 Keep Physical Copies: Checkposts at Janki Chatti (Yamunotri), Gangotri town entry, Sonprayag (Kedarnath), and Pandukeshwar (Badrinath) scan your registration. Digital display of the registration is accepted at most posts, but carry a printout as backup \u2014 mobile signals at checkposts can be weak.\nSenior Citizen Medical Certificate \u2014 Get it Before Departure: The Char Dham Shrine Board mandates a medical fitness certificate for pilgrims above 60 at the Haridwar biometric camp. The certificate must be from a registered medical practitioner and dated within 30 days of the registration date. Getting this before leaving Kolkata avoids delays at the camp.",
    "highlights": [
      "Har Ki Pauri Ganga Aarti, Haridwar \u2014 Attend the evening Aarti at the most sacred Ghat on the Ganga. Our team positions the group at the upper-tier viewing area \u2014 not the crowded lower steps \u2014 30 minutes before the 6:45 PM ceremony begins.",
      "Yamunotri Temple \u2014 Surya Kund ritual \u2014 Trek 6 km from Janki Chatti (2,650m) to the temple at 3,291m, or opt for pony/palki. Cook raw rice in the boiling Surya Kund hot spring (104\u00b0C) as prasad \u2014 a ritual specific to Yamunotri that no other Dham offers.",
      "Gangotri on the Bhagirathi \u2014 Temple at 3,048m at the banks of the Bhagirathi, with views of the Himalayan glacier valley. Less footfall than Kedarnath; Darshan is unhurried. Our group reaches by 10:00 AM \u2014 well before the 2:00 PM midday closure.",
      "Kedarnath Jyotirlinga \u2014 VIP queue coordination \u2014 Our ground coordinator pre-registers the group for priority entry at this Jyotirlinga. General queue wait time in peak season: 3\u20135 hours. With coordination: 45\u201390 minutes.",
      "Kedarnath Abhishek at 5:00 AM \u2014 The early-morning abhishek on Day 9 is the most intimate darshan experience on the entire circuit \u2014 fewer than 200 people, direct view of the lingam. Most packages miss this because they do not overnight at Kedarnath.",
      "Badrinath + Mana Village, last Indian village \u2014 After Badrinath darshan, walk 3 km to Mana \u2014 the last inhabited Indian village before the Tibet border. Visit Vyas Gufa (where the Mahabharata was dictated), Bhim Pul (natural rock bridge over the Saraswati), and the Saraswati River that vanishes into the earth.",
      "Devprayag \u2014 Where the Ganga is Born \u2014 Stop at the confluence of the Alaknanda and Bhagirathi on Day 12. This is the precise geographical point where the river officially becomes the Ganga. Most groups drive past; we stop for 20 minutes.",
      "Triveni Ghat Aarti, Rishikesh \u2014 Final evening aarti at Rishikesh's Triveni Ghat as the tour concludes \u2014 a spiritually complete ending to the circuit."
    ],
