  "kailash-yatra-form-kathmandu": {
    "overview": "Experience the glory and magical power of Kailash Mansarovar as you join us on the Kailash Mansarovar Yatra from Kathmandu. While curating itineraries, we keep in mind all the aspects of the tour.\n\nWe focus on offering safe, comfortable, and well-organized yatra experiences with excellent ground support, good-quality accommodations, travel permits, expert guidance, and high-altitude assistance. Whether you're travelling for the first time or you have undertaken the Kailash Mansarovar Yatra before, you're bound to have the best experiences with us. \nWhy choose the Kailash Mansarovar Yatra from Kathmandu in 2026?\nKailash Mansarovar Yatra is not just an ordinary journey, but a spiritual voyage that takes you closer to the path of salvation. And we make this quest for enlightenment and spiritual fulfillment easier.\n\nWe have extensive experience in the industry and are trusted by our clients due to our well-organized itineraries, excellent arrangements, and guided tours.\nWe take care of all the essentials such as travel permits, travelling assistance, accommodation, and food.\nOur Kailash Mansarovar package from Kathmandu is organized in well-managed groups for better coordination and care.\nOur support staff and guides are always there to help you during the journey. Your safety is our number one concern.\n\nKey Highlights of the Package\n\nGuided pilgrimage to Kailash Mansarovar, allowing you to soak in the sacred vibes of the region.\nYour tour begins in Kathmandu (Nepal). The briefing covers altitude awareness, route, and health check.\nPilgrims often drink water, take ritual baths, and spend time at the Mansarovar\u2019s lakeside and reflect under Himalayan skies.\nThe most rewarding part of the Kailash Yatra via Kathmandu is the parikrama around sacred Mount Kailash.\nYou\u2019ll meet local nomad communities, pass Tibetan monasteries, witness high-altitude hospitality, and perform many rituals.\n\nBest Time for Mansarovar Yatra starting from Kathmandu\n\nThe Kailash Mansarovar route typically opens in May for all tourists. If you want to be the first to avoid the overwhelming crowd, consider undertaking the Yatra in May.\nIn June, the skies are clear, so the landscapes and mountain vistas are mesmerizing in this month. With the start of June, you will find the temperature to be pleasant.\nJuly and August are considered to be the peak monsoon period in China, Tibet, and India. However, Kailash Mansarovar receives light rainfall during the monsoon. Most pilgrims undertake the yatra in July and August due to the full moon period and Hindu festivals, which makes doing Kailash kora even more auspicious.\nSeptember is considered to be a pleasant time for the Kailash Mansarovar Yatra. September brings stability and peace after the peak pilgrimage period.\nOctober marks Kailash Mansarovar Yatra\u2019s end. The weather gets very cold during this month.\n\nHow to Prepare for the Yatra?\n\nSpend some time at a lower altitude like Shigatse, Nepal-China border (Gyirong), or Lhasa before ascending to high altitude. Give yourself ample time to acclimatize.\nStart preparing for the yatra at least two to three months before the travel date. Go for slow jogs or brisk walks. You can also do breathing exercises such as pranayama.\nLight strength training is important, especially for the core and lungs. This will help you a lot during the Parikrama.\nInvest in good quality gear that keeps you comfortable, warm, dry, and safe. Get sturdy hiking boots that have a good grip.",
    "highlights": [
      "Guided pilgrimage to Kailash Mansarovar, allowing you to soak in the sacred vibes of the region.",
      "Your tour begins in Kathmandu (Nepal). The briefing covers altitude awareness, route, and health check.",
      "Pilgrims often drink water, take ritual baths, and spend time at the Mansarovar\u2019s lakeside and reflect under Himalayan skies.",
      "The most rewarding part of the Kailash Yatra via Kathmandu is the parikrama around sacred Mount Kailash.",
      "You\u2019ll meet local nomad communities, pass Tibetan monasteries, witness high-altitude hospitality, and perform many rituals."
    ],
    "itinerary": [
      {
        "title": "Day 1 :Arrival in Kathmandu",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 2 :Kathmandu \u2013 Nepalgunj",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 3 :Nepalgunj \u2013 Simikot",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 4 :Simikot \u2013 Hilsa / Purang",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 5 :Purang Local Sightseeing",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 6 :Purang \u2013 Mansarovar",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 7 :Mansarovar to Darchen",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 8 :Darchen \u2013 Deraphuk",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 9 :Deraphuk \u2013 Zuthulphuk",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 10 :Zuthulphuk \u2013 Hilsa / Simikot",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 11 :Simikot / Nepalgunj \u2013 Kathmandu",
        "description": ""
      },
      {
        "title": "Day 12 :Kathmandu Departure",
        "description": ""
      }
    ],
